[Music]  hello everybody and  welcome to my talk on voice  uh my name is meg rozoff and i'm an  american writer living in  london and i started writing quite  late in life and  once i started teaching creative writing  i realized that i couldn't teach people  to write but i could  teach them to at least try  uh to think properly or to think like a  writer and that's when i started to get  this idea about voice  now everybody talks about voice um but  it's very  difficult to actually define what it is  the first question that i usually ask  and the first question that i  asked myself uh when i first  started getting sent books by publishers  was why are so many books so  dead um i used to think that  in order to write a good book all you  had to do was have a good plot  and to be able to string a sentence  together so  i would pick up books that had been sent  to me by publishers and i would think  well yes i'll have a look at this  it's bound to be good i know the person  they're quite articulate or i don't know  the person but  you know it looks beautiful it's got a  good cover um  and time after time i found myself  feeling that there was  just something missing in the book  and i started thinking of them as zombie  books  now the thing that really does tend to  be missing in a voi  in a book is a strong voice  um the word is misleading  as i've written here uh voice suggests a  superficial quality  but it's not a superficial quality voice  is in fact the essence  of your personality it's the clearest  expression of your dna  plus a lifetime of experience so what is  the combination of nature  and nurture add up to in your head  what you're looking for is the one thing  that you can say  that nobody else can say um  i find this is something that uh that  people don't  really have an easy idea thinking about  you go to look at paintings in a gallery  for instance and you look at the mona  lisa and you feel nothing  now that's always blamed on you it's  your fault you can't somehow react to  the mona lisa  but maybe it's the mona lisa's fault  maybe lisa are not  compatible maybe the voice of that  artist  is not a voice that speaks to you  but as an artist what you need is to  have the voice in the first place so  that at least you're speaking to  somebody  now i started thinking about  the brain as a as a colander  and i started to think about how single  day  there will be a million experiences  more than a million a billion  experiences you wake up you open your  eyes you put your  feet on the floor you go you have  breakfast a billion million things  happen to you  and most of them just go straight  through the holes of the calendar  you'll never remember them if i asked  you what were you doing at 7 11 in the  morning  uh a week ago tuesday  chances are you wouldn't be able to tell  me um  but every once in a while and really  almost every once in a blue moon  something happens in your day in your  life  that gets stuck in the colander and  sticks there and may still be in that  colander  10 years later 20 years later 50 years  later  there's a question that therapists ask  when you go  in one of the first questions they ask  is what's your first memory  um and if you ask people that question  just routinely you get an extraordinary  variety  of of first memories my first memory i  was very very young and it was a dream  and it was a nightmare in fact  and if you dumped that colander with all  the things  that stuck in your head out onto a big  white table  at any age at age 11 or 18 or 35 or  60 you would notice that what's in your  colander  is completely different from what's in  the person's next to use calendar  because what's stuck in their brain  even if you had an identical upbringing  will not be  remotely the same and this is why the  writing of memoir is so difficult  because i was talking about this  um the other day um  because two people identical twins in a  family  will have completely different  experiences growing up  even if their dna is the same their what  they observe in life how they're reacted  to  uh by the parents by the other siblings  by  life will be completely different  now one thing i do think is quite  interesting is that  school um especially in this country in  in the uk  is really designed to  erase out um the individuality the  difference you know you're all passing  the same exams you're all wearing the  same uniforms  um and uh it's it's almost as if we have  to educate people  back into their idiosync their  idiosyncrasies  and uh their faults in a way you know  you  you're always told you have to be good  that you have to be you know  soft-spoken and polite and you know  honest and  fair dealing and you know ev  as if everyone should be the same and if  you happen to have an explosive  passionate  you know nutty personality um  it's often not valued as much or if  you're  not very outgoing it's often not valued  as much people are always saying  smile or you know why don't you talk up  more  uh but that's what you are that's what's  in your head and that's what you really  need to treasure above all things  so just imagine all these kinds of  things anybody who's a gardener out  there will recognize this  this is all the kind of stuff that's  collecting in your brain and it's  composting down into this really  rich dark uh material  let's call it material because when  you're writing a book uh that's where  the book comes from  another example is is the my my favorite  train example  and what i like to do if i've got a an  actual live audience in front of me is  to say we've got a  we've got a train that's half a mile  long and everybody  here today is sitting in the  this in the left hand window of the  train  you're all looking out at the same  landscape as it's passing by at speed  and every single person on that trip  will notice something different we'll  take away something different from that  trip so i always say that what i notice  is is animals i i'll be looking for  horses  uh out the window if i can't find a  horse you know uh  i would get special extra points for a  deer or if i were in america a moose  imagine seeing a moose out the window  um i'm always looking for hairs in  fields  which you can't see if you take a train  in march april you know you do often see  i once saw two boxing hairs in a field  and that made my  journey absolutely extraordinary um  you know somebody else might be looking  at thinking about property  you know looking at the houses that you  pass and saying well i'd quite like to  live there or  i'd really like to have a garden like  that um  you know someone else might be looking  at trees or looking for birds or  not looking at all uh looking down at  the book in their lap  or you know at the person sitting across  from them on the aisle  but basically everyone's sitting in the  same place on the same  journey it's going to have a different  experience and the  quality the the difference between your  experience and the experience of the  person  next to you or the other people in your  life is what's going to make your story  interesting  now if you don't know what's in your  calendar if you don't know what's in  your  head you need to find out um  i was just reading this over this  morning  and i thought well that's interesting  the interpretation of dreams is the  royal road to a knowledge of the  unconscious activities of the mind well  it must have been freud who said that  um dreams are one of the best ways to  get  into your unconscious mind because it's  the one place where  the the the deep dark unconscious  pokes its way up to the surface so  if you think about it and a lot of  people haven't ever thought about it  if you think about it the idea that you  go to sleep and you shut off your  conscious mind uh and some of your brain  is telling you stories and it's not  telling you  kind of normal stories it's not saying  you know when you go to work tomorrow uh  you might want to think about  cleaning your desk it's saying you know  uh you know there's a deer in a tree  and uh but the deer has wings and  um i i don't know why the deer is right  then suddenly i go up to the deer to  talk to it and it flies off  um and people don't seem to uh  don't seem to kind of recognize how  completely peculiar it is  that to continue watching this video  click the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai dot tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas

